  add('matching', { title: 'Set 12: Second-hand traders', items: [
    { type: 'text', title: 'Four people describe selling second-hand goods', numbered: false, paras: [
      `<strong>A</strong> I started by accident. Three years ago I passed a skip outside a house in my street and saw an oak dining chair with a broken leg sticking out of it. I took it home, glued it, rubbed it down and, to my own surprise, sold it for more than my week's shopping budget. Since then I have restored a few hundred pieces and sell them from a unit behind the garage. The odd thing I have learnt is that people rarely ask whether a table is perfect. They ask where it came from. A scratch with a history sells; a flawless piece with none sits there for months. So I keep a folder for each item with every scrap I can discover about its previous owners. I also say no to dealers who want a bargain to flip, since I would rather it ended up in someone's kitchen. People assume that this is about nostalgia; it is mostly about stubbornness.`,
      `<strong>B</strong> I sell second-hand books from a spare bedroom, and the arithmetic is unforgiving. Most books fetch a couple of pounds, so I earn a living only because I list and post several hundred a month, with the kind of wrapping and weighing that nobody who buys a paperback pictures. What keeps me going is what turns up between the pages: a bus ticket from 1974, a pressed flower, a shopping list, once a love letter used as a bookmark. I have started to keep the better finds in a drawer, and I sometimes wonder what the writers would make of readers who treasure their margins. People tell me that electronic readers will finish me off. I doubt it, because nobody keeps a pressed flower in a file.`,
      `<strong>C</strong> When I first dressed my son in clothes that another baby had worn, I felt vaguely ashamed, as if I had failed to give him something new. I would never have admitted that to the other mothers at the school gate, who were doing exactly the same thing with pride. Now I run an online swap for children's clothes, with about two hundred families, and I look back at that embarrassment with amusement. What I care about now is the quality of what circulates. I have one firm rule: nothing that was clearly made to last a single season, however fashionable. A jacket that survives four children has paid for itself, whereas a flimsy one passed round simply spreads a problem. Members sometimes grumble, but the rule has made the swap's reputation.`,
      `<strong>D</strong> I have a stall on the Saturday market selling old records and radios, and I learnt the trade expensively. In my first month I bought a crate of unlabelled cables from a man who swore they were rare, and I spent a weekend discovering that they were ordinary ones, worth nothing. Since then I do not pay more for anything than I could afford to lose, and I walk away from sellers who hurry me. The customers have also changed. Ten years ago people came for a bargain. Now most of the young ones ask me whether the player can be repaired, and they say they would rather pay more than throw a thing away. I am not sure whether it is fashion or conscience, but it suits me. My weekends are long, but nobody has ever asked me to attend a meeting on a Saturday.`
    ] },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person was at first uncomfortable about giving a child things that were not new?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 2, why: `C: "felt vaguely ashamed" at dressing her son in another baby's clothes. The embarrassment is C's alone; D's early trouble was a bad purchase, not a feeling about used goods.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person began trading as a result of rescuing something that had been thrown away?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 0, why: `A: the chair in the skip, glued and sold. B and D sell second-hand goods but say nothing of how they began.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person made an expensive early mistake by relying on someone else's word?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 3, why: `D: the crate of cables bought from "a man who swore they were rare". A mentions saying no to dealers, but that concerns selling, not an early mistake.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person regularly comes across personal items left behind by earlier owners?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 1, why: `B: the bus ticket, pressed flower and love letter "between the pages". A researches the previous owners deliberately, not by chance discovery in the goods.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person finds that the history of an item matters more to buyers than its condition?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 0, why: `A: "A scratch with a history sells; a flawless piece with none sits there for months." B treasures finds but never says that buyers pay for history.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person thinks that buyers' reasons for choosing second-hand goods have shifted from saving money to principle?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 3, why: `D: ten years ago "people came for a bargain", now the young "would rather pay more than throw a thing away". C's members share clothes, but C describes no change in their motives.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person can make a living only by handling a very large number of cheap items?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 1, why: `B: "Most books fetch a couple of pounds", so B lists and posts "several hundred a month". A sells a few hundred pieces over three years, at far higher prices.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person refuses to accept goods made to be used for a short time only?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 2, why: `C: "nothing that was clearly made to last a single season". A turns away certain buyers (dealers), not certain goods.` }
  ] });

  add('matching', { title: 'Set 13: Amateur astronomers', items: [
    { type: 'text', title: 'Four people describe their hobby of watching the sky', numbered: false, paras: [
      `<strong>A</strong> I live in a town where the sky glows orange all night, so every clear weekend I load the telescope into the car and drive an hour and a half to a farm with no street lights. My wife thought it was a midlife crisis, and for the first year she made little effort to hide it. Then, one night, she looked at Saturn's rings through the eyepiece, said nothing for a minute, and has come with me ever since, bringing a flask and a firm opinion about the best place to sit. Dark skies are the whole secret of the hobby. A modest telescope at a good site shows more than an expensive one in a city, and I think that is why I do not mind the miles. I keep a log of everything I see, partly from habit and partly because the pages make a pleasing record of nights that would otherwise run together.`,
      `<strong>B</strong> I am a retired engineer, and I made my telescope. It took me fourteen months to grind and polish the mirror by hand, checking its shape with a test that is sensitive to a fraction of the wavelength of light. A mirror like that cannot be hurried. Buying one would have been quicker and probably better, but I would have missed the thing I enjoyed most. I now spend most clear nights measuring the brightness of variable stars and sending my figures, as hundreds of others do, to an international archive that researchers use. I do not imagine that anyone will notice one night's observation, but the archive is the sum of thousands. Friends ask whether I mind that a shop-bought mirror would have been finer; I tell them that the finest instrument is the one you understand completely.`,
      `<strong>C</strong> I teach physics to thirteen-year-olds, and the astronomy club I run is the only lesson in which nobody asks what the point is. When pupils see the moons of Jupiter with their own eyes, they stop treating science as something that happens elsewhere, to other people. The difficulty is the weather. In this country, a night on which the club can actually observe is rare, so I plan every session in two halves: one under the sky if possible, and the other in the classroom with a projected simulation and a set of star charts. Last term the sky cooperated twice. The children were delighted both times, and, I suspect, so was I. Parents sometimes write to say that their children now look up on the way home, which is the best result any teacher can have.`,
      `<strong>D</strong> My pictures take hours. I point a camera at a patch of sky and combine hundreds of exposures, each too faint to show anything alone, until a galaxy appears that no eye could see in a lifetime. People find this less romantic than looking through an eyepiece, but I find it the more honest, because it shows what is really there. I am impatient with hobbyists who spend thousands on equipment and then colour the results until they look like a poster. A picture that is a pleasing fiction is useful to nobody. I would rather produce something dull and true than something spectacular that was never in the sky. It is slow work, and most of my evenings are spent at a laptop rather than outdoors, but the result is worth the wait.`
    ] },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person believes that a photograph can reveal what the naked eye never could?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 3, why: `D: a galaxy "that no eye could see in a lifetime". A says that a modest telescope shows more at a dark site, but that is about location, not about a picture.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person regularly spends a long time travelling to find suitable conditions?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 0, why: `A: "drive an hour and a half to a farm with no street lights". C also struggles with conditions, but by planning for poor weather, not travel.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person contributes data that professional scientists can draw on?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 1, why: `B: figures sent "to an international archive that researchers use". D produces pictures but never says they are submitted to anyone.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person has a back-up plan for when the weather spoils a session?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 2, why: `C: each session has a second half "in the classroom with a projected simulation". A is willing to drive far for clear skies but makes no back-up plan.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person made the main instrument instead of buying one?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 1, why: `B: "I made my telescope... grind and polish the mirror by hand". D mentions expensive equipment only in criticising other hobbyists.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person saw a relative's early doubt turn into shared enthusiasm?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 0, why: `A: the wife who thought it "a midlife crisis" and then "has come with me ever since". C's pupils change their view of science, but they are not relatives.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person criticises other enthusiasts for caring more about looks than truth?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 3, why: `D: hobbyists who "colour the results until they look like a poster". B regards buying a mirror as quicker but does not criticise anyone.` },
    { type: 'mcq', q: `Which person uses the hobby to change young people's view of science?`, options: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], answer: 2, why: `C: pupils "stop treating science as something that happens elsewhere, to other people". B contributes to research but is not shown teaching or influencing anyone.` }
  ] });
